{{short description|American physician}}
{{other people|Daniel Peterson}}

'''Daniel Peterson''' is an [[Americans|American]] [[physician]] in private practice in the state of [[Nevada]], and has been described as a "pioneer" in the treatment of [[Myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome]] (ME/CFS).<ref name="Patient Zero">{{cite book | last1 = Verrillo | first1 = Erica F. | first2 = Lauren M. | last2 = Gellman | title = Chronic Fatigue Syndrome: A Treatment Guide | publisher = Macmillan | year = 1998 | page = 152 | url = https://books.google.com/books?id=VtHlTVfToiQC&q=peterson+first+used+ampligen+in+cfs&pg=PA152 |isbn=978-0-312-18066-9 }}</ref><ref name="HHV6BOD">{{cite web |title=HHV-6 Foundation Board of Directors |publisher=HHV-6 Foundation |year=2004 |url=http://hhv-6foundation.webs.com/boardofdirectors.htm |access-date=2009-03-18 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100128193723/http://hhv-6foundation.webs.com/boardofdirectors.htm |archive-date=2010-01-28 }}</ref> He graduated from the [[University of Rochester School of Medicine]], [[Rochester, New York]], in 1976 and was an intern and resident at the [[University of Utah Medical Center]] from 1976 to 1979. In 1979, he became a diplomate of the [[American Board of Internal Medicine]].<ref name="WPInst">{{cite web |title=executive staff |publisher=[[Whittemore Peterson Institute|The Whittemore Peterson Institute]] |year=2008 |url=http://www.wpinstitute.org/about/about_execboard.html |access-date=2009-01-11 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091011025958/http://www.wpinstitute.org/about/about_execboard.html |archive-date=2009-10-11 }}</ref> He is president of Sierra Internal Medicine of [[Incline Village]], established in 1981.<ref name="Sierra">{{cite web  | title = Sierra Internal Medicine | publisher = [[Dun & Bradstreet]] | year =2008 | url = http://www.manta.com/coms2/dnbcompany_f4xfgj  | access-date = 2009-01-12}}</ref>

==Work in Myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome==

Along with Paul Cheney, Peterson was a treating physician at Incline Village during an outbreak of ME/CFS that began in 1984 in the [[Lake Tahoe]] region.<ref name="PTL">{{cite video | people = Sam Donaldson, Nancy Snyderman, Paul Cheney, David Bell, [[Elaine DeFreitas]], Hillary Johnson, PWC's, Paul Pollard, Mrs. Dailor, Philip Lee | title = Sick & Tired | medium = Television | publisher = ABC News |date =1996-03-27}}</ref> From 1984 to 1987, the illness was recorded in 259 patients in the area by the two physicians. The Lake Tahoe outbreak became the subject of several studies by Peterson and others. In 1995, Peterson and other investigators started conducting a 10-year follow-up study on patients seen during the outbreak. The study results were published in 2001 by the ''Journal of Chronic Fatigue Syndrome''.<ref name="Strickland">{{cite journal|first1=Paula S. |last1=Strickland |first2=Paul H. |last2=Levine |first3=Daniel L. |last3=Peterson |first4=Karen |last4=O'Brien |first5=Thomas |last5=Fears |title=Neuromyasthenia and Chronic Fatigue Syndrome (CFS) in Northern Nevada/California: A Ten-Year Follow-Up of an Outbreak |journal=Journal of Chronic Fatigue Syndrome |volume=3/4 |series=9 |pages=3–14 |year=2001 |url=http://dceg.cancer.gov/cgi-bin-pubsearch/pubsearch/index.pl?page=abstract&ID=6563&project=dceg |doi= 10.1300/J092v09n03_02|url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090201150557/http://dceg.cancer.gov/cgi-bin-pubsearch/pubsearch/index.pl?page=abstract&ID=6563&project=dceg |archive-date=2009-02-01 |url-access=subscription }}</ref> In the 2000 CFS documentary [[I Remember Me]], Peterson was interviewed about some of his experiences during the Lake Tahoe outbreak.<ref name="Ebert">{{cite web | last = Ebert | first = Roger | author-link = Roger Ebert | title = I remember Me | work = Movie Review | publisher = Roger Ebert Chicago Suntimes | date = December 7, 2001 | url = http://rogerebert.suntimes.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=/20011207/REVIEWS/112070301/1023 | access-date = 2009-01-07 | archive-date = 2008-02-10 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20080210173957/http://rogerebert.suntimes.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=/20011207/REVIEWS/112070301/1023 | url-status = dead }}</ref>

In 1988, Peterson was the first physician to treat an extremely ill person diagnosed with ME/CFS with the [[experimental drug]] [[Ampligen]] by obtaining compassionate-use permission from the [[U.S. Food and Drug Administration]]. [[quantitative property|Quantitative]] improvement in the first patient enabled the next pilot study of Ampligen in ME/CFS patients by Peterson and other researchers. During the 1990 CFIDS Conference in [[Charlotte, North Carolina]], Peterson described positive results in 15 CFS patents after he treated them with Ampligen for approximately 6 months.<ref name="Patient Zero"/> In 1990 and 1991, Peterson was one of four principal investigators for the FDA approved phase II randomized [[placebo]] controlled [[Blind experiment#Double-blind trials|double-blind]] study of the experimental [[intravenous]] drug Ampligen. The drug was administered in his Incline Village facility and three other sites.<ref name="Amp II">{{cite book |last = Johnson | first = Hillary |title=Osler's web: inside the labyrinth of the chronic fatigue syndrome epidemic |publisher=Crown Publishers |location=New York |year=1996 |page= 385 |isbn=978-0-517-70353-3 }}</ref> Peterson and others reported that there was statistically significant improvement in the patients receiving Ampligen.<ref name="Amp II results">{{cite journal | last1 = Strayer | first1 = D.R. | last2 = Carter | first2 = W.A. | last3 = Brodsky | first3 = I. |title=A controlled clinical trial with a specifically configured RNA drug, poly(I).poly(C12U), in chronic fatigue syndrome |journal=Clin. Infect. Dis. |volume=Suppl 1 |pages=S88–95 |series=18 |date=January 1994 |pmid=8148460 |doi= 10.1093/clinids/18.supplement_1.s88|display-authors=etal}}</ref> He is a [[principal investigator]] of the FDA-approved open-label safety and efficacy phase III drug study of Ampligen for treatment of ME/CFS.<ref name="Amp 511">{{cite web | title = Study of Ampligen in Chronic Fatigue Syndrome | publisher = U.S. National Institutes of Health |date=September 2005 | url = http://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT00215813 | access-date = 2009-01-06}}</ref> Hemispherx Biopharma's [[New Drug Application]] for marketing and sale of Ampligen to treat ME/CFS was rejected in December 2009 because the FDA concluded that the two RCTs "did not provide credible evidence of efficacy."<ref name="PBJ2010-02-12">{{Cite web|url=http://philadelphia.bizjournals.com/philadelphia/stories/2009/11/30/daily23.html|title=FDA rejects Hemispherx's chronic fatigue drug Ampligen|last=George|first=John|date=December 3, 2009|publisher=Philadelphia Business Journal|access-date=6 April 2020}}</ref>

Peterson was a member of the International Chronic Fatigue Syndrome Study Group that coauthored the most widely used clinical and research description of CFS,<ref name = Wyller>{{cite journal | last = Wyller | first = V.B. |title=The chronic fatigue syndrome--an update |journal=Acta Neurologica Scandinavica. Supplementum |volume=187 |issue= s187|pages=7–14 |year=2007 |pmid=17419822 |doi=10.1111/j.1600-0404.2007.00840.x | s2cid = 11247547 |doi-access=free }}</ref> called the 1994 CDC definition, and the [[Keiji Fukuda|Fukuda]] definition.<ref name="CDC1994">{{cite journal | last1 = Fukuda | first1 = K. | last2 = Straus | first2 = S. | last3 = Hickie | first3 = I. | last4 = Sharpe | first4 = M. | last5 = Dobbins | first5 = J. | last6 = Komaroff | first6 = A. | title = The chronic fatigue syndrome: a comprehensive approach to its definition and study. International Chronic Fatigue Syndrome Study Group. | journal = Ann Intern Med | volume = 121 | issue = 12 | pages = 953–9 | year = 1994 | pmid=7978722| doi=10.7326/0003-4819-121-12-199412150-00009| s2cid = 510735 }}</ref> He is a coauthor of ''Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome: Clinical Working Case Definition, Diagnostic and Treatment Protocols'', initiated by [[Health Canada]] and published by an international group of researchers in 2003.<ref name="carr">{{cite journal|last1=Carruthers |first1=B.M. |title=Myalgic encephalomyalitis/chronic fatigue syndrome: Clinical working definition, diagnostic and treatment protocols |journal=Journal of Chronic Fatigue Syndrome |volume=11 |issue=1 |pages=7–36 |year=2003 |url=http://www.cfids-cab.org/MESA/ccpccd.pdf |doi=10.1300/J092v11n01_02 |display-authors=etal |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081216222746/http://www.cfids-cab.org/MESA/ccpccd.pdf |archive-date=2008-12-16 }}</ref><ref name="pmid 16935963">{{cite journal | last = Carruthers | first = B.M. |title=Definitions and aetiology of myalgic encephalomyelitis: how the Canadian consensus clinical definition of myalgic encephalomyelitis works |journal=J. Clin. Pathol. |volume=60 |issue=2 |pages=117–9 |date=February 2007 |pmid=16935963 |doi=10.1136/jcp.2006.042754 |pmc=1860613}}</ref><ref name="isbn 9780750688673">{{cite book | last1 = Khot | first1 = Alex | last2 = Polmear | first2 = Andrew | title = Practical General Practice: Guidelines for Effective Clinical Management | publisher = Elsevier Health Sciences | date = 24 April 2006 | pages = 419–421| isbn = 978-0-7506-8867-3 | edition = 5th New }}</ref>

Peterson, along with Annette and [[Harvey Whittemore]], helped establish the [[Whittemore Peterson Institute]] (WPI) for Neuro-Immune Disease at the [[University of Nevada, Reno|University of Nevada]] in 2005 to aid patients with chronic fatigue syndrome, [[fibromyalgia]] and related illnesses.<ref name=RGJ_01_11_2009>{{Cite news
  | last = Powers
  | first = Lenita
  | title = Daughter's illness led family to seek help, start institute
  | newspaper = Reno Gazette-Journal
  | date = 1 November 2009
  | url = http://www.wpinstitute.org/news/docs/RGJ-daughters_illness.pdf
  | access-date = 10 February 2010
  | url-status = dead
  | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20101202045453/http://wpinstitute.org/news/docs/RGJ-daughters_illness.pdf
  | archive-date = 2 December 2010
  }}</ref> In October 2009 Peterson was interviewed on [[National Public Radio]] about his views on ME/CFS and the newly published possible association with the retrovirus [[XMRV]].<ref name="isbn NPR_Oct2009">{{Cite news | last = Montagne | first = Renee | title = Virus Tied To Chronic Fatigue Syndrome | newspaper = National Public Radio| date = October 9, 2009  | url = https://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=113650222 | access-date = 2010-03-06}}</ref>

In 2010, Peterson left WPI for personal reasons. He stated there was a lack of collaboration with him over the research direction of the institute concerning XMRV.<ref name=Nature032011>{{Cite journal | last = Callaway | first = Ewen | title = Virology: Fighting for a cause | journal = Nature | volume = 471 | issue = 7338 | pages = 282–85 | date = 14 March 2011 | doi = 10.1038/471282a | pmid = 21412308 | bibcode=2011Natur.471..282C| doi-access = free }}</ref> Peterson then teamed with [[Jay Levy]], one of the original discoverers of HIV, to try to determine whether XMRV is truly present in patients by testing the same patients used in the study published in ''Science''.<ref name=Nature032011/> They did not find indications of XMRV in the blood of the patients tested, and also concluded from their experiments that XMRV does not, "survive well in human blood", so human infection is unlikely. They also stated that research results published by others suggested that laboratory contamination may have resulted in false positive results in the original study.<ref name=LAT_09_30_2011>{{Cite news
  | last = Jay A. Levy and Daniel L. Peterson
  | title = The mystery of chronic fatigue syndrome
  | newspaper = Los Angeles Times
  | date = September 30, 2011
  | url = https://www.latimes.com/opinion/la-xpm-2011-sep-30-la-oe-levy-chronic-fatigue-syndrome-20110930-story.html
  | access-date = 10 February 2011}}</ref>

==Affiliations and awards==

Peterson is a member of the board of directors and the scientific advisory board of the HHV-6 Foundation,<ref name="HHV6BOD"/> a non-profit organization promoting [[human herpesvirus 6]] (HHV-6) associated scientific and clinical research.<ref name="NINDS">{{cite web | title = HHV-6 Foundation| publisher = National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke| url = http://www.ninds.nih.gov/find_people/voluntary_orgs/volorg863.htm| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20060930042358/http://www.ninds.nih.gov/find_people/voluntary_orgs/volorg863.htm| url-status = dead| archive-date = September 30, 2006| access-date = 2009-02-15}}</ref> Peterson was part of the founding board and is a past-president of the International Association for CFS/ME,<ref name="WPInst"/> a professional organization advocating for the interests of ME/CFS researchers and clinicians worldwide.<ref name="pmid 17662750">{{cite journal | last1 = Friedberg | first1 = F. | last2 = Sohl | first2 = S. | last3 = Schmeizer | first3 = B. |title=Publication trends in chronic fatigue syndrome: comparisons with fibromyalgia and fatigue: 1995–2004 |journal=J Psychosom Res |volume=63 |issue=2 |pages=143–6 |date=August 2007 |pmid=17662750 |doi=10.1016/j.jpsychores.2007.03.003 }}</ref><ref name=timesinterview>David Tuller, "Expert Q&A, Learning Firsthand about Chronic Fatigue Syndrome" Interview with Leonard Jason, {{cite web |url=http://health.nytimes.com/ref/health/healthguide/esn-chronicfatigue-expert.html |title=Chronic Fatigue - Expert Q & A - Learning Firsthand About Chronic Fatigue Syndrome - NY Times Health |access-date=2012-04-26 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091013184432/http://health.nytimes.com/ref/health/healthguide/esn-chronicfatigue-expert.html |archive-date=2009-10-13 }} April 30, 2008</ref>

In 2003, he received the Rudy Perpich award, an award given to distinguished CFS/FM scientists, physicians or healthcare workers, and in 2007, received the Nelson Gantz Outstanding Clinician Award from the International Association for CFS/ME.<ref name="IACFS/ME awardees">{{cite web | title = Former IACFS/ME awardees | publisher = International Association for CFS/ME | url = http://www.iacfsme.org/FormerIACFSAwardees/tabid/209/Default.aspx | access-date = 2009-01-07 | url-status = dead | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20090103091848/http://www.iacfsme.org/FormerIACFSAwardees/tabid/209/Default.aspx | archive-date = 2009-01-03 }}</ref>

In 1999, Peterson was commended by the Assembly and Senate of the State of Nevada for his work and dedication to persons with ME/CFS.<ref name="AJR14">{{cite web | title = AJR14| publisher = Nevada Legislative Counsel Bureau | date = April 8, 1999 | url = http://www.leg.state.nv.us/Session/70th1999/bills/AJR/AJR14_EN.html| access-date = 2010-10-13}}</ref>

